  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHi-resolution convective models have grown increasingly\nconfident that the convergence of strong 850mb warm advection\n(associated with MCS activity to the west) and weak shortwave\nadvection within northwest flow through the New England region\nresults in scattered thunderstorm development this evening\nmainly in northwest PA. \n\nThe environment will feature enough CAPE (generally between\n800-1000 J/kg) and shear (25-35kts) to offer low probability\ndamaging wind and hail threats. Those risks are mainly before\n8pm as nocturnal cooling reduces buoyancy enough to sap at that\npotential. Of more interesting is a growing potential for some\nstorm convergence along a stalled warm axis oriented NW-SE\nsomewhere east of I-79 along the I-80 that could prolong storm\nduration, resulting in flash flooding. Though it could shift\nslightly west/east, this axis favors north central Mercer County\nthrough the Clarion/Armstrong border and has Hi-ref peak totals\nthat could exceed 2\", enough to cause flooding on saturated\ngrounds. This risk lingers towards the midnight hour before\nenough push from the mid-level NW shortwave drags the storms out\nof the region southeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nPrevious discussion of the next flood risk arriving Thursday \nhas been severely undercut by the 12z of model data that \nsuggests enough high pressure and shortwave ridging builds \nThursday morning to slow the progression of the next NW wave \nrounding the southern ridge. Subdued PoPs help highlight this as\nthat risk appears to have shifted either overnight Thursday \ninto Friday or at some point in the day Friday. For now, note \nthat a shortwave combined with overrunning warm advection again \nfavors the development of thunderstorms along a fairly \nstationary axis that results in heightened flash flood risks.\n\nLike many previous discussions note, this pattern has featured a\nlot of uncertainty that may be highly influenced by the previous\n6-24 hours of convective activity. This late Thursday/Friday\nperiod will be no different, meaning timing of these storms\nalong with positioning of the axis could see large variations\nleading up the event. More simply, there are scenarios where\nstorms occur at some point that drop multiple inches of rain\nover some portion of the region (resulting in flash flooding)\nwith other portions are wet a lot of the day, OR there are\nscenarios where the region remains completely dry. As always, \nstay tuned for the latest updates for this period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe overall synoptic pattern only sees minor variations this\nweekend into next week as the predominant flow setup (ridge\nsouth to southwest of region; zonal to northwest flow overhead)\nstays in tack. Periodic showers and thunderstorms, generally\nshorter in duration compared to this past week, remain possible\nin such flow while temperature largely stays near seasonal\naverages. Saturday is the more likely day of break from\nactivity, but even that scenario could adjust as models attempt\nto better time shortwave movement/timing.\n\nInitial glance from ensemble means as well as publicly available\nmachine-learning models suggests more limited severe/hydro risk\nthan the previous week. But it remains prudent to follow the\nlatest trends as there are (low probability) scenarios still in\nplay that bring one or both more prominently into play.",
